=========================================================
CLASS TEMPLATES (C++11/14/17)
Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is a Class Template?
============================

A class template allows writing a single generic class that can work with
different data types.

Instead of creating separate classes for int, double, string, etc., one
generic class can be written.

Example (Without Templates)

class IntBox
{
    int value;
};

class DoubleBox
{
    double value;
};

Problem
-------
• Duplicate code
• Difficult to maintain

---------------------------------------------------------

Using Class Templates

template<typename T>
class Box
{
    T value;

public:
    Box(T val) : value(val) {}

    T getValue() const
    {
        return value;
    }
};

Usage

Box<int> b1(10);

Box<double> b2(3.14);

Box<string> b3("Hello");

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Syntax
=========

template<typename T>
class ClassName
{
    // members
};

---------------------------------------------------------

3. How Class Templates Work
===========================

A class template is only a blueprint.

The compiler generates the required class when it is instantiated.

Example

Box<int> box1;

Compiler Generates

class Box<int>
{
    int value;
};

Similarly,

Box<double> box2;

Compiler Generates

class Box<double>
{
    double value;
};

This process is called

Template Instantiation

---------------------------------------------------------

4. Multiple Template Parameters
===============================

A class template can have multiple template parameters.

Example

template<typename K, typename V>
class Pair
{
public:

    K key;
    V value;

    Pair(K k, V v)
        : key(k), value(v)
    {
    }
};

Usage

Pair<int,string> p(10,"Apple");

---------------------------------------------------------

5. Member Functions Inside Class
================================

template<typename T>
class Box
{
public:

    void print()
    {
        cout << "Value";
    }
};

---------------------------------------------------------

6. Member Functions Outside Class
=================================

Syntax

template<typename T>
ReturnType ClassName<T>::FunctionName()

Example

template<typename T>
class Box
{
public:

    Box(T value);

    T getValue();

private:

    T value;
};

template<typename T>
Box<T>::Box(T val)
    : value(val)
{
}

template<typename T>
T Box<T>::getValue()
{
    return value;
}

Important

Always write

Box<T>::

instead of

Box::

---------------------------------------------------------

7. Multiple Template Parameters Outside Class
=============================================

template<typename K, typename V>
class Pair
{
public:

    Pair(K,V);

private:

    K key;
    V value;
};

template<typename K, typename V>
Pair<K,V>::Pair(K k, V v)
    : key(k), value(v)
{
}

---------------------------------------------------------

8. Default Template Arguments
=============================

Template parameters can have default types.

Example

template<typename T = int>
class Box
{
    T value;
};

Usage

Box<> b1;

Equivalent to

Box<int> b1;

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Non-Type Template Parameters
===============================

A class template can also accept constant values.

Example

template<typename T, int SIZE>
class Array
{
private:

    T data[SIZE];
};

Usage

Array<int,10> arr;

Compiler Knows

Type = int

Size = 10

---------------------------------------------------------

10. Static Members in Class Templates
=====================================

Each template instantiation has its own static member.

Example

template<typename T>
class Counter
{
public:

    static int count;
};

template<typename T>
int Counter<T>::count = 0;

Usage

Counter<int>::count++;

Counter<double>::count++;

These are DIFFERENT variables.

---------------------------------------------------------

11. Static Member Functions
===========================

template<typename T>
class Counter
{
public:

    static void display()
    {
        cout << "Counter";
    }
};

Usage

Counter<int>::display();

---------------------------------------------------------

12. Friend Functions
====================

Friend functions can also be templates.

Example

template<typename T>
class Box
{
    T value;

public:

    Box(T v)
        : value(v)
    {
    }

    template<typename U>
    friend void show(Box<U>);
};

template<typename U>
void show(Box<U> obj)
{
    cout << obj.value;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

13. Template Class Objects
==========================

Every object must specify a template argument.

Example

Box<int> b1;

Box<double> b2;

Box<string> b3;

---------------------------------------------------------

14. Why Template Implementations are in Header Files?
=====================================================

Templates are instantiated during compilation.

The compiler must see the complete implementation.

If implementation is kept only in .cpp files,

Linker errors occur.

Therefore,

Templates are usually implemented completely inside header (.h/.hpp) files.

---------------------------------------------------------

